The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (((struct smc_clc_msg_decline *)buf)->hdr.typev2 & SMC_FIRST_CONTACT_MASK) {
    smc->conn.lgr->sync_err = 1;
    smc_lgr_terminate_sched(smc->conn.lgr);
}
```
Reason:
When a CLC decline message arrives before the connection is associated with a link group, `smc->conn.lgr` is NULL. The original code unconditionally dereferences `smc->conn.lgr` without checking for NULL, causing a null pointer dereference and a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if ((dclc->hdr.typev2 & SMC_FIRST_CONTACT_MASK) && smc->conn.lgr) {
    smc->conn.lgr->sync_err = 1;
    smc_lgr_terminate_sched(smc->conn.lgr);
}
```
Reason:
The fix adds a guard `&& smc->conn.lgr` to ensure that the link group state is only updated when a valid link group exists. This prevents accessing a NULL pointer and keeps the per-socket peer diagnosis handling unchanged, while preserving the sync_err logic for established link groups.

